WATCH OUT

  - Claim the paths you edit in the shared clone. See AGENTS.md for
    lane scopes, version bumps and tests. CHECKLISTS.md is a separate
    shared claim when minting TCK IDs.
  - Generated corpus indexes trail page commits by one; commit them
    after the pages, then rerun make corpus.
  - Interactive pager whatis tests in tests/run.sh use the small
    fixture guide, not the live corpus. Verify new glossary names with
    a separate TAM_KNOWLEDGE_DIR=knowledge PTY check.
  - The pager status line shows a page title, not its path. Assert a
    page's own body marker when checking a whatis resolution.
  - Cross-page links in corpus pages are root-relative from knowledge/,
    not relative to the current page. Breadcrumb `index.html` is the
    Guide root link.
  - A page title belongs in <title> and the breadcrumb only. Do not
    add a body heading that repeats the breadcrumb; start with an
    intro paragraph. The root guide index has no breadcrumb and keeps
    its <h1>.
  - A `<dfn>` needs a glossary link inside the element, not around it.
  - Glossary links must match the sense in the page. The existing
    glossary defines Core as a CPU unit, Session as a login session,
    Capability as a Linux permission bit, Filter as a Unix filter,
    Service as a background program, Paging as memory paging and
    Current as a glossary title about the latest state. Do not link
    those words in carrier, chat, spam or electrical contexts by reflex.
  - make corpus regenerates glossary hubs from tools/glossary_index.py,
    so heading changes there must be made in the generator too.
  - Glossary pages carry one definition per level and link to
    teaching pages. Put detailed distro comparisons in the Operating
    Systems Reference, not in a glossary entry.
  - Acronym glossary titles spell the expansion in parentheses, and
    the breadcrumb repeats the full title. Preserve paired titles such
    as BPF / eBPF and avoid nested parentheses.
  - Glossary pages are filed by the first letter of their title. If a
    check reports a misplaced page, run
    `python3 tools/corpus_pipeline.py check --fix-glossary`: it moves
    the page, fixes its parent and breadcrumb, rewrites inbound links
    and regenerates the hubs. Digit-leading titles belong in
    glossary/numeric/.
  - Bibliography IDs must start with a letter. Prefix numeric standards
    such as 3GPP with `spec-` in filenames, `data-ref` values and links.
  - A path stage's step count includes checkpoint links carrying
    `?level=`. Keep answer prose free of levelled page links or reduce
    the numbered steps to stay within four.
  - Every meta skills ID must exist in knowledge/skills/skills.json;
    register a new domain at all three levels before building.
  - A step's `meta paths` ID must equal the path page's `meta topic`;
    otherwise the corpus build reports an unknown learning path.
  - Exact keyword matches outrank title and field matches in whatis.
    If a teaching and a glossary page share one exact keyword, the
    earlier index entry wins; vary the teaching page's wording.
  - Sandbox <tam-cmd match> is a literal glob string, not a shell
    expression: it does not expand `$$` or variables. Author exact
    output rules for those commands, and use &quot; for quotes inside
    a double-quoted match attribute.
  - Pipe rendering shows a sandbox's goal and <tam-init> line, not
    its command log. Keep the init line short so it does not wrap
    awkwardly inside the box.
  - A sandbox shows at most the last eight log entries; PTY assertions
    on multi-line scripted output must not rely on the first row still
    being on screen.
  - For an interactive PTY check of a sandbox deep in a page, search
    with `/Sandbox: <title>` then press Right; repeated Down keys can
    run the attention line past the sandbox.
  - Lb is a lead line: it takes one content row at the top of the pager
    and reduces the numbered-line range shown there by one. Pager row
    and scroll assertions must account for it. Generated views built
    with tam_page_render() do not carry Lb unless they inject it
    themselves; depth-prepared corpus pages get it from
    tam_depth_prepare().
  - The status row repeats a selected link label with the link's own
    reverse styling. Raw-PTY assertions that search for `\x1b[7m`
    must scope themselves to page rows, not the whole buffer.
  - Interactive checks of generated views need `tam show <view>`, not
    a bare `tam <view>` alias; only some views have bare aliases.
  - PTY assertions on coloured rows must strip ANSI escapes first: a
    right-aligned Lb can be split by colour codes. Wait for a content
    marker instead of a fixed delay when the view loads an index.
  - Inline python3 -c blocks in tests/run.sh are single-quoted: avoid
    apostrophes in comments or strings inside them.
  - The reveal and hide keys r and a are reliable for first-time
    asides; Right Arrow depends on where the attention line sits.
  - Tamlinux's desktop plan is its own compositor, tam-pane. Sway and
    wlroots are peer projects or technical examples, not targets.
  - Tamlinux's host/rootstock/bud/scion/cultivar/fruit naming is a
    botanical analogy, not a set of plant names.
  - Fold notices are transient st->msg text: a movement key clears
    them only when the key did not write a new notice, so reveal-all
    still reports its count.
  - Command and corpus versions are independent; corpus-only work does
    not bump the command version.
  - UBSan output in a PTY corrupts the pager screen model; log it to a
    file when testing under sanitizers.
  - work/history/ is read-only; new sessions use work/W<n>-<name>.
  - make dev is Fred's loop; agents do not run it. Do not install a
    build as a side effect of content changes.
